1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:06,547
In 1692, Richard Coman told a Salem court about a night no one else could see.

2
00:00:06,497 --> 00:00:08,971
He said the house was locked.

3
00:00:08,971 --> 00:00:11,115
A light was burning.

4
00:00:11,115 --> 00:00:13,628
His wife lay beside him.

5
00:00:13,628 --> 00:00:16,326
Then Bridget Bishop entered the room.

6
00:00:16,326 --> 00:00:24,852
Coman said she lay across his chest and pressed him down so completely that he could neither speak nor move—not even enough to wake his wife.

7
00:00:24,852 --> 00:00:29,207
Years after the night itself, he put that memory under oath.

8
00:00:29,207 --> 00:00:33,497
Eight days after his testimony, Bridget Bishop was hanged.

9
00:00:33,497 --> 00:00:37,089
Coman's statement did not create the case against her.

10
00:00:37,089 --> 00:00:42,010
It entered a much larger prosecution already crowded with accusations.

11
00:00:42,010 --> 00:00:51,694
But it shows something extraordinary: a private experience had crossed into a courtroom, attached itself to a living woman, and become part of public reality.

12
00:00:51,694 --> 00:00:55,800
The record cannot tell us what state produced Coman's night.

13
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:58,668
It tells us what Salem did with it.

14
00:00:58,668 --> 00:01:07,286
So how does an experience no one else can witness become evidence—and can the explanation a culture gives that experience change the next night?

15
00:01:07,286 --> 00:01:10,168
Look closely at Coman's sequence.

16
00:01:10,168 --> 00:01:12,971
He is awake in a familiar room.

17
00:01:12,971 --> 00:01:14,984
The door is locked.

18
00:01:14,984 --> 00:01:17,036
A figure appears.

19
00:01:17,036 --> 00:01:19,207
His voice fails.

20
00:01:19,207 --> 00:01:21,615
His body will not answer.

21
00:01:21,615 --> 00:01:24,497
Pressure settles across his chest.

22
00:01:24,497 --> 00:01:27,813
Today, the pattern is recognizable.

23
00:01:27,813 --> 00:01:32,405
But Salem had no language of REM sleep or muscle atonia.

24
00:01:32,405 --> 00:01:35,760
It had another language ready: witchcraft.

25
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:38,760
And Salem did not invent the pattern.

26
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:43,115
In 1781, Henry Fuseli painted The Nightmare.

27
00:01:43,115 --> 00:01:46,221
A woman lies thrown back across a bed.

28
00:01:46,221 --> 00:01:49,142
A squat figure sits on her chest.

29
00:01:49,142 --> 00:01:54,628
From behind a curtain, a horse stares into the room with white, impossible eyes.

30
00:01:54,628 --> 00:02:00,050
The painting became famous not because it explained the night, but because it gave the night a body.

31
00:02:00,050 --> 00:02:04,431
Across Europe, the nightmare was once more than a bad dream.

32
00:02:04,431 --> 00:02:10,971
The old word carried weight: the mare, or mara, a visitor that rides or presses the sleeper.

33
00:02:10,971 --> 00:02:14,036
Christian Europe imagined the incubus.

34
00:02:14,036 --> 00:02:19,102
In Japan, people spoke of kanashibari—being bound as if by metal.

35
00:02:19,102 --> 00:02:22,931
In Newfoundland, the visitor became the Old Hag.

36
00:02:22,931 --> 00:02:29,155
Other traditions gave pressure, paralysis and nocturnal presence their own agents and names.

37
00:02:29,155 --> 00:02:32,865
These figures do not form one clean family tree.

38
00:02:32,865 --> 00:02:37,221
No single demon can be followed from one language to the next.

39
00:02:37,221 --> 00:02:39,892
What repeats is more basic.

40
00:02:39,892 --> 00:02:41,905
The room is known.

41
00:02:41,905 --> 00:02:43,997
The body is trapped.

42
00:02:43,997 --> 00:02:46,260
Something approaches.

43
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:48,589
Pressure gains intention.

44
00:02:48,589 --> 00:02:54,155
Perhaps the story begins because the body produces an experience that demands a cause.

45
00:02:54,155 --> 00:03:00,128
But once a culture has supplied that cause, the next person does not enter the night empty-handed.

46
00:03:00,128 --> 00:03:07,497
In early modern Europe, a nocturnal attack belonged to a world of sin, spirits, curses and divine protection.

47
00:03:07,497 --> 00:03:16,852
If the cause was an acting being, the response had to be an action: prayer, a cross, iron, water, a household object placed near the bed.

48
00:03:16,852 --> 00:03:20,628
From the outside, these acts can look irrational.

49
00:03:20,628 --> 00:03:24,089
From inside that world, they are practical.

50
00:03:24,089 --> 00:03:28,865
If the ritual brings relief, the explanation feels confirmed.

51
00:03:28,865 --> 00:03:31,865
That is how the bedroom opens outward.

52
00:03:31,865 --> 00:03:35,036
An episode can support a family story.

53
00:03:35,036 --> 00:03:37,036
A religious order.

54
00:03:37,036 --> 00:03:39,023
An accusation.

55
00:03:39,023 --> 00:03:42,681
In Salem, it could enter the machinery of a court.

56
00:03:42,681 --> 00:03:48,050
The simplest modern answer would be that people see the beings their culture taught them to see.

57
00:03:48,050 --> 00:03:51,760
Then David Hufford creates a problem for that answer.

58
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:55,615
Hufford studied the Old Hag tradition in Newfoundland.

59
00:03:55,615 --> 00:03:58,905
He also knew the experience personally.

60
00:03:58,905 --> 00:04:06,707
What mattered most to his work, however, were people who described the familiar pattern before they knew the local story that named it.

61
00:04:06,707 --> 00:04:08,576
Paralysis.

62
00:04:08,576 --> 00:04:10,036
Pressure.

63
00:04:10,036 --> 00:04:12,036
Presence first.

64
00:04:12,036 --> 00:04:14,063
The Old Hag later.

65
00:04:14,063 --> 00:04:17,668
Culture did not have to invent the entire event.

66
00:04:17,668 --> 00:04:24,247
A recurring human state could come first, and communities could build different explanations around it.

67
00:04:24,247 --> 00:04:30,550
That helps explain why the same bodily structure can appear in traditions with no simple line of descent.

68
00:04:30,550 --> 00:04:33,155
But it opens a harder question.

69
00:04:33,155 --> 00:04:38,247
If culture does not have to create the first episode, what can it do to the second?

70
00:04:38,247 --> 00:04:42,142
Imagine two people waking into the same paralysis.

71
00:04:42,142 --> 00:04:44,878
One knows the state will pass.

72
00:04:44,878 --> 00:04:51,839
The other has heard since childhood that this is the moment a real being attacks—and that people can die in its grasp.

73
00:04:52,814 --> 00:04:55,801
The body may begin in the same place.

74
00:04:55,751 --> 00:04:58,422
Does the night end in the same place?

75
00:04:58,422 --> 00:05:04,896
A research team led by Baland Jalal and Devon Hinton compared reports from Egypt and Denmark.

76
00:05:04,896 --> 00:05:10,869
Sleep paralysis appeared in both settings, but its meaning did not arrive in the same form.

77
00:05:10,869 --> 00:05:17,488
In the Egyptian sample, supernatural explanations—especially jinn—were far more common.

78
00:05:17,488 --> 00:05:22,067
In the Danish sample, physiological explanations were more common.

79
00:05:22,067 --> 00:05:27,172
These were specific samples, not portraits of two entire countries.

80
00:05:27,172 --> 00:05:30,659
Yet the difference inside the data was striking.

81
00:05:30,659 --> 00:05:38,948
Egyptian participants in the studies reported more lifetime episodes, longer immobility and greater fear of dying during an attack.

82
00:05:38,948 --> 00:05:47,238
Within the Egyptian group, believing that a supernatural force caused the paralysis was associated with greater fear and longer immobility.

83
00:05:47,238 --> 00:05:50,461
Association is not a one-way spell.

84
00:05:50,461 --> 00:05:55,080
The studies do not show that belief simply manufactures paralysis.

85
00:05:55,080 --> 00:06:01,132
They show something more intimate: interpretation and bodily distress can become entangled.

86
00:06:01,132 --> 00:06:03,540
Fear can fracture sleep.

87
00:06:03,540 --> 00:06:07,277
Fractured sleep can make another episode more likely.

88
00:06:07,277 --> 00:06:10,606
Another episode can feel like confirmation.

89
00:06:10,606 --> 00:06:14,488
The expectation waiting for the next night grows heavier.

90
00:06:14,488 --> 00:06:17,080
The story has entered the loop.

91
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:21,790
And that may be the most unsettling possibility in this entire history.

92
00:06:21,790 --> 00:06:27,961
A being does not have to stand physically in the room for belief in it to produce physical consequences.

93
00:06:27,961 --> 00:06:30,619
So return to Richard Coman.

94
00:06:30,619 --> 00:06:35,830
His body supplied wakefulness, immobility, pressure and presence.

95
00:06:35,830 --> 00:06:40,448
Salem supplied a name, a cause and a court willing to hear it.

96
00:06:40,448 --> 00:06:43,882
The experience moved outward into testimony.

97
00:06:43,882 --> 00:06:47,435
The testimony returned to the community as truth.

98
00:06:47,435 --> 00:06:49,488
What comes first?

99
00:06:49,488 --> 00:06:53,159
For one person, perhaps the experience.

100
00:06:53,159 --> 00:06:58,251
For a culture, experience and story may already be braided together.

101
00:06:58,251 --> 00:07:01,593
Every new night meets images that are waiting.

102
00:07:01,593 --> 00:07:05,580
Every new account changes what the next person expects.

103
00:07:05,580 --> 00:07:09,948
That is why the question is not simply whether the visitor was real.

104
00:07:09,948 --> 00:07:19,580
The deeper question is what kind of reality a visitor can acquire—inside a body, inside a memory, and finally inside a room full of other people.

105
00:07:19,580 --> 00:07:24,988
Which carries more power into the next night: the EXPERIENCE, or the STORY?

106
00:07:24,988 --> 00:07:27,330
Leave one word below.

107
00:07:27,330 --> 00:07:30,067
Because the body may open the door.

108
00:07:30,067 --> 00:07:34,777
But once a culture gives the darkness a face, that face can travel.

